Correct this carefully.

Apply these rules:

1. One actual garment design should have one product listing.
2. Different photographs of the same garment belong to the same product gallery.
3. The same design in genuinely different colours may be managed as variations.
4. Different garment designs must remain separate products.
5. Renaming an identical garment does not create a new product.
6. Images should not be unnecessarily duplicated.
7. Stock must not be double-counted when consolidating confirmed duplicates.

Inspect the current catalogue.

Use actual available images, product information, variations and other reliable identifiers to identify likely duplicates.

Do not delete products based only on similar names or colours.

Two garments with the same colour may have completely different designs.

If you cannot confidently establish whether two products are identical, preserve both and report them for owner confirmation.

Before any consolidation:

- Identify the records involved.
- Identify the correct product record to retain.
- Identify the associated images.
- Identify variations and stock.
- Check historical order references.
- Preserve necessary historical data.
- Use a safe and reversible approach where possible.

Do not bulk-delete products.

Do not execute old seed files to recreate the catalogue.

